# Envy preview workflow

Use the `envy-compose` skill distributed with Envy for composition lifecycle and
frontend bindings. Fill in these repository-specific values before use:

- Envy project: <registered project>
- Baseline: <registered baseline>
- Components owned by this repository: <component IDs>
- Registered source repository ID and build-report location: <mapping and CI artifacts>
- Task composition ID / saved recipe location: <task handoff or ignored file path>
- Preferred preview TTL and cleanup point: <duration and when to stop>
- Build and publish images: <exact repository commands>
- Immutable image reference/provenance: <where to obtain digest and commit>
- Frontend name and HTTPS repository URL: <name>, <URL>
- Frontend URL build variable and API path: <variable>, </graphql or API path>
- Frontend build command and hosting integration: <commands>
- Application tests and browser flows: <commands, paths, expected behavior>
- Shared test-data rules: <permitted data and cleanup>
- API credentials: <approved secret manager or ignored token file; never values>

Coordinate using an explicit composition ID, not a branch or display name. One
coordinator submits complete override updates with the expected generation.
Infrastructure readiness and passing application/browser checks are separate.
Never fall back to staging when exact frontend revision resolution fails.

Choose no environment, explicit baseline use, reuse, update or replacement based
on the task's actual tests. Do not create compute merely because a PR exists.
Switching frontend revisions can reuse the backend composition. Adding/removing
backend override components currently needs a replacement composition. Save a
recipe when work will outlive its TTL; recreate with fresh bindings and evidence.
